Whoaa! Hold on a minute! In a post earlier today, Harry credited me with
launching this pledge drive, so I want to make sure y'all understand
my views.

I don't see any discussion going on about the idea of
"member/nonmember" mailing lists, just a call to send money.

Greed being what it is, when I send money in I want to know I'm not
subsidizing "freeloaders." (No insult to the nonmembers, you
understand.) Just another donation to ExI is not what I was talking about!

Now maybe the member/nonmember software is not the software that is
ready now, or that is desired. But let's at least debate it, shall we?

In the meantime, I have done what I said I would do, namely, send a
check for $30 directly to rjc for his efforts. (Of course, I mailed it
with a one-cent stamp, so....)

If there is to be a new pledge drive, without any discussion of truly
making the list a "pay for what you get" list, then fine. But this is
not what I proposed, and I don't see what the point is, except as a
general pledge drive (which, not to sound picky, I had understood
there would be no more of, ever?).

I'm all for giving money to worthy causes, but I despair of having a
small minority of a few dozen (the folks listed as contributors in the
newsletter) sending in pledges/donations while the other hundred or
more subscribers contribute nothing. As I cannot "force" others to
contribute (for various reasons, be they moral, practical, or PPL), I
favor making the list a real market, to thus incentivize
contributions.

With a new pledge drive--perish the thought!--I'll be very surprised
if we get anywhere near the levels reached a year ago, when Tom Morrow
so ably, and uncomfortably, exorted us on to reach the "one time only"
special emergency goal. With a pay-for-what-you-get list, on the other
hand, I could easily see an eventual phase transition to the majority
of the list getting the "member" version...and I suspect that this
will greatly exceed the pledge drive levels (for so many reasons). It
will also be a *recurring* revenue source, as presumably many folks
will resubscribe each year to the "member" list (whereas asking them
every year for just one more pledge drive donation is irritating,
unseemly, and causes resentment).

Let's debate this issue, not just launch a new pledge drive!

How Could It Work?

It seems to me that the mechanics of a "Phase One" member/nonmember
dichotomy system are simple, or could be made simple. To wit:

- all incoming mail goes to one source (the "reply-to" field)

- mail to "nonmembers" is handled by one list that acts just like the
"old" software did: simple reflection. No ::excludes, ::includes, etc.
This might actually be the new software, with all special processing
turned off or commented out. (If this is impractical, then just use
the old software.)

- mail to "members" (which might be the $30 ExI membership plus a $20
e-mail membership, or $40 for the e-mail membership only) would go
through the full list software in all its glory.

I think the bookkeeping would be fairly straightforward, and easily
done by hand. Upon confirmation of membership (by whooever takes the
checks), the list manager would transfer the subscriber from the
default nonmember list to the member list.

There would be two lists, but the total number of outgoing messages
would be constant.

Of course, perhaps the same list software could handle both members
and nonmembers, and give them differing capabilities. But in case this
is not possible, the "two lists" approach is a straightforward fallback.

By the way, another completely different tack to take is to let
newcomers on the list (perhaps just one list) for some "trial
subscription" period, perhaps 90 days. At the end of this period,
they would be expected to join the ExI. (I'll hold off commenting on
this further.) If not, they're off the list.

In closing, I don't want to see our List turn into another
"guiltathon" pledge drive-based system, like PBS, with exortations
that we donate (and with no sanctions whatsoever for those who don't).
"From each according to his guilt, to each the very same subscription"
is not very appealing.

Let's make it a real market. It shouldn't be hard at all.


-Tim

