Date: Sat, 18 Sep 93 03:08:15 GMT
From: sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk (Stephen J. Whitrow)
Subject: GUNZ: "Pro-gun" is not equal to "pro-violence"

"Jay R. Freeman" says:

> Andy Lowton's presence prompts me to ask whether there are British
> libertarians, and if so, whether their feelings on gun control are as
> strong as the feelings of their American counterparts?

When an area of land has been taken over by a powerful monopolist
criminal organisation, which coerces the local inhabitants into doing
as they are commanded --  obeying often irrelevant, centrally imposed
'laws' which arise from the whims of the masters, paying dues, or even
dying -- it clearly makes sense from the organisation's point of view to
employ armed agents in order to maintain its grasp of power, whilst
denying the natives the right to keep and bear arms.  But from the
natives' angle, it would seem to me to be not a smart move to tip the
balance of power still further in favour of this organisation which
calls itself "the Government".  Or to refrain from seeking more power
for the natives.   

The balance of power between two groups, and the rights that either
group may command, has a lot to do with the balance of firepower between
both groups.  When one compares the number of killings and atrocities
carried out by the agents of governments with the number carried out by
private individuals, I don't think this provides much of a case for
concentrating such power in the hands of a privileged minority.  Power
corrupts pro-rata to its extent.  Since weapons will be in the hands of
some group, no matter what, I believe respectable individuals would be
safer if respectable individuals were allowed to bear arms, rather than
having arms concentrated in the hands of criminals. 

By "criminals" I am including anyone who fails to observe the rights
that cooperating humans would tend to devise and reciprocate in order to
maximise utils per capita -- such as property rights, the right not to be
mown down, and in fact the right not to have force initiated against 
oneself without consent.  Governments would have people believe that they
are a special case: they and their agents should be able to ride
roughshod over the people's rights, supposedly for the 'benefit of the
whole'.  But it appears very likely that governments do not 'benefit the
whole' for a number of reasons:

*  The 'top-down' or downward causation approach works very well for an
   object assembled in a very complex manner, like a computer or a human
   brain, where the total is much more than the sum of its parts.  The
   object's raison d'etre is the function that is carried out at the very
   highest level, which generates information and extropy.  But a
   society of humans does not self-organise to become analogous to a
   brain.  The individuals are the most important parts of it; hence the
   top-down method of central controls fails.  

*  Corruption / abuse of power / plain sadism.  

*  Inefficiency: for every unit that is spent on some public 'good' 
   another unit is wasted along the line.  Poor 'value for money'. 

*  Unintended consequences: these are mostly beneficial when
   individuals voluntarily cooperate to promote their own interests --
   a spontaneous order acts for the good of the whole, so upward
   causation works.  But the state cannot possibly predict all of
   the unintended consequences arising from its action, e.g. lowering  
   of incentives thus reducing output.  And these are almost certainly
   harmful.
      
*  Rather than acting purposefully towards their chosen goals,
   individuals are ordered to pick their fourth choice / twelfth choice
   / ninety-fifth choice action.  Free individuals would select as
   their first choice that action which yielded them the most utils,
   therefore their total utils are lowered by government.

Governments have the effect of lowering per capita utils.  Basically
governments benefit themselves at the expense of those who are external
to them.  But the tragedy is that most of the people believe that 
governments actually benefit them.  It should be possible for all
those external to government to cooperate together in a mutually
beneficial arrangement which would gain back that which has been lost
to governments.  (And to police the arrangement, part of it would 
include provision for a realignment between former govt. supporters
and the former government -- if the voluntary agreements failed.)

The idea of refusing to let one's own side bear arms brings to mind the
time a few years ago when some representatives of the British Labour
Party returned from Moscow "cock-a-hoop" over a brilliant deal that
they'd clinched with the Soviets.  The arrangement was that Britain
would destroy all its nuclear weapons, and in return the Soviets would
"point theirs in a different direction".  Fortunately the Labour Party
was not in power, but it was quite funny at the time.  (Still, we get
plenty of amusement from the Major Government, what with incidents like
the "Bastardgate Tapes" and the toe-sucking "Minister of Fun".)

As for the deaths of a few football hooligans, I too would welcome it;
they will find it rather difficult to reoffend, and the quality of the
average human gets raised.  

So by depriving citizens of the right to keep and bear arms, this firstly
provides an advantage to 'conventional' outlaws, as already mentioned by
others.  But even more important could be the effect in strengthening
governments.  The risk of totalitarianism over the next few decades,
particularly during a period of very rapid technological transition
would be increased, and the opportunities for establishing PPL in the
time before the Singularity would be reduced.

A fine post by Mark Grant by the way, I enjoyed it.

Steve Whitrow                           sjw@liberty.demon.co.uk

P.S. It was recently said that Government assurances on the safety of
six-year-old frozen beef were simply a load of old bull.  As to who 
would be the most gullible -- those who took the previous sentence
literally, those who believed the Government, or those who believed
that dictionary editors refused to include "gullible" -- the jury
appears to be still out. 

