Re: Dirty Pair

The manga (comic) version licenced to Studio Proteus/Dark Horse
(former partnership: Studio Proteus/Eclipse) by Adam Warren and Toren Smith,
is to be highly recommended.

The most recent 4-part series, "Sim Hell" is set in an extremely
well-conceived VR.  The artwork and story are both _superb_.
And intelligent.

Interestingly, one of the viewpoint characters, Kei, seems to
have, at one point, gotten stuck in an historical simulation of (what
is then) the aftermath of an epoch known in their timeline as "the
Nanoclysm", a "Blood Music"-style near-apocalypse.  In a
followup issue, in the letters section, the writer explicitly
acknowledges /Engines of Creation/ as an influence.

The artwork is very densely textured, and, if you look closely,
you'll see clever little references to "Ayoob" (gun nuts - my
crowd - will recognise that one), "Hogan", and "Heinlein", among
others.

The politics is of a proto-libertarian, Heinleinian,
anti-bureaucratic type.

The technology is sound: Lagrangian space habitats and the like.

I would recommend starting from the first book in the series,
/Biohazards/, then /Dangerous Acquaintances/, then /A Rage of
Angels/, before /Sim Hell/.

There is amazing improvement in the continuum from Book 1 to
Book 4.

